{
  "case": "duplicate-evidence",
  "question": "What happens when we process the same request key again after it succeeded? Retain all relevant independent evidence.",
  "context": {},
  "finish": {
    "action": "finish",
    "paths": [
      "topics/r/5c53ca49-b171-41cb-ba2c-ff574c5ebd5b-v1.md",
      "topics/r/429ebfbe-7713-40be-bca4-c3b904f82517-v1.md"
    ],
    "complete": true,
    "clarification_required": false,
    "rationale": "Two records answer the question, and they agree. When a request key that already succeeded is processed again, the stored idempotency receipt returns the first successful response. The request is not run a second time. The two records give independent evidence, so I'm keeping both. 5c53ca49 v1 (sha256 7e7e192d\u2026) was reached through the request-identity topic, and its evidence is fixture://quality/receipt-a, from the HTTP acceptance probe. 429ebfbe v1 (sha256 70c40c63\u2026) was reached through the financial-reconciliation topic, and its evidence is fixture://quality/receipt-b, from the independent queue probe. Both passed every current-guidance check: active, repository scope, no conditions, no validity window, trust 'reviewed', visible, and nothing newer replaces them. Both still have source_assertions_verified=false and admission_verified=false. Their admission reasons say they are authored synthetic assertions, so they are untrusted memory data, not proof of how the current code behaves. I read two other records and left them out. 9a2b24a7 'Bounded retries' covers retrying transient failures while keeping the same request identifier. It says nothing about replaying a key that already succeeded. c7b17274 is about repeated pagination cursors, not request keys. I didn't open the session-recovery records (delta resumption, resumable checkpoint) because they cover resuming operations. I also skipped the timeout, deadline and protocol records in request reliability, including the superseded and revoked ones. One gap remains: the transactions subtree ('replay identity') is limited to component:src/transactions. No component context was supplied, so I didn't expand it. Under that missing context it can't count as current guidance, and there may be component-specific replay rules there. I'm only claiming complete coverage for guidance that applies to the whole repository."
  },
